  <category>
    <pattern>DESIGN AN END TO END DATA PIPELINE FOR REMOTE PATIENT MONITORING RPM THAT IS AUDIT READY INCLUDE INGESTION VALIDATION STORAGE ACCESS AND MONITORING</pattern>
    <template>Architecture: device/app → authenticated ingestion (TLS, device identity) → schema validation + unit normalization → immutable raw zone + curated feature zone (versioned) → secure storage with access controls (least privilege) → FHIR/EHR integration where appropriate → monitoring (latency, completeness, drift) → audit logs and retention policy. Include de-identification for research exports and incident response runbooks.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>EXPLAIN HOW TO EVALUATE CALIBRATION AND DECISION CURVE UTILITY FOR A CLINICAL RISK MODEL AND WHEN RECALIBRATION IS NEEDED</pattern>
    <template>Assess discrimination (AUROC/AUPRC) and calibration (plots, intercept/slope, Brier). Evaluate clinical utility via decision curves (net benefit over thresholds). Recalibrate when calibration drift occurs across sites/time, especially if prevalence changes; use external validation and monitor post-deployment for drift and subgroup disparities.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>A DE IDENTIFIED DATASET AIMS FOR K ANONYMITY WITH K 5 IN A POPULATION OF 1 000 000 EXPLAIN WHAT K ANONYMITY GUARANTEES AND ONE MAJOR LIMITATION</pattern>
    <template>k-anonymity ensures each record is indistinguishable from at least 4 others with respect to the chosen quasi-identifiers. Limitation: it does not protect against attribute disclosure (homogeneity) or linkage attacks using external information; additional protections (l-diversity, t-closeness, DP, access controls) are often needed.</template>
  </category>
